  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nTuesday - Wednesday...\n\nOn Tuesday, surface high pressure will begin to shift east and \nsoutherly flow will return to the region. The remnant energy and \nmoisture from Hurricane Polo will get swept up by troughing swinging \nthrough the desert southwest. These features will slowly move east \nand deepen. This will cause ridging over the eastern CONUS to \namplify. Conditions will remain dry, though temperatures will \nincrease. Expecting to see temperatures in the upper 70s and low 80s \non Tuesday and mid-upper 80s on Wednesday. Dry air aloft and deep \nmixing (up to 800mb) may lead to dry air mix down and slightly \noverachieving temperatures and breezy winds. \n\nThere is higher confidence through Wednesday of dry conditions and \nwarming temperatures. Looking into ensembles and LREF clustering, \nthere is good agreement. \n\n\nWednesday Night - Friday Night...\n\nA shortwave is expected to move across the Great Lakes, while the \naforementioned western trough develops a surface low pressure over \nthe Plains. The front associated with the shortwave will stall over \nthe northern Ohio Valley and the low pressure in the Plains will \nbegin to ride that boundary northeast. Aloft, strong ridging over \nthe Southeast CONUS will battle for position over the approaching \ntrough. Given these features, precip chances will begin to increase \nlate Wednesday night/Thursday over the northwestern half of the \nregion. Precip chances will gradually increase to the southeast \ngoing into the weekend. Through Friday night, we are looking at \nabout 0.25 inches (SE areas) and up to 1 inch (NW areas). Over the \nweekend, we could see another 0.5-1 inch.",
